Your implementation is efficient and can handle a large amount of text. It uses the `fileread` function to read the text files, which is more memory-efficient than reading the entire content into memory at once. The `lower` function converts all words to lowercase, making the comparison case-insensitive. The `strsplit` function splits the text into words, and the `setdiff` function finds the unique words that do not appear in both sets. Finally, the `disp` function prints the unique words.

To further optimize the code, you can use the `memmap` function to map the text files into memory, which can be faster than using `fileread`. You can also use the `parfor` loop to parallelize the computation, which can take advantage of multiple cores or processors. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to use `memmap` and `parfor`:
```matlab
text1 = memmap('Text1.txt', 'r'); %read text1 into memory
text2 = memmap('Text2.txt', 'r'); %read text2 into memory
%Convert text to lowercase and split into words
words1 = lower(strsplit(text1));
words2 = lower(strsplit(text2));
%Find unique words in text1 that do not appear in text2
uniqueWords = setdiff(words1, words2);
%Print the unique words
disp(uniqueWords);
```
