In the Dining Philosophers Problem, four philosophers share two chopsticks to eat. They can either pick up their left chopstick or right chopstick, but they need both chopsticks to eat. This scenario can lead to a deadlock if certain conditions are met:

1. Mutual Exclusion (Only one philosopher can use the chopsticks at a time)
2. Hold and Wait (A philosopher holds one chopstick while waiting for another)
3. No Preemption (Once a philosopher has picked up a chopstick, he cannot release it)
4. Circular Wait (A sequence of philosophers exists where each is waiting for a chopstick held by the next philosopher in the sequence)

In this setting, all four conditions for deadlock hold true:

1. Mutual Exclusion: Only one philosopher can use the chopsticks at a time.
2. Hold and Wait: A philosopher holds one chopstick while waiting for another.
3. No Preemption: Once a philosopher has picked up a chopstick, he cannot release it.
4. Circular Wait: A sequence of philosophers exists where each is waiting for a chopstick held by the next philosopher in the sequence.

To avoid deadlocks, we can eliminate any one of these four conditions:

1. Mutual Exclusion: Allow multiple philosophers to use the chopsticks simultaneously.
2. Hold and Wait: Allow philosophers to release chopsticks they are not using.
3. No Preemption: Allow philosophers to release chopsticks they have picked up.
4. Circular Wait: Break the circular wait by having philosophers prioritize which chopstick they will pick up first.

