```matlab
% Define the initial population
population = [0.7 0.3]; % Frequency of the advantageous trait
generations = 100; % Number of generations

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:generations
    % Calculate fitness based on the advantageous trait
    fitness = population(1) * 1.5; % Advantageous trait increases fitness by 50%

    % Generate offspring using roulette wheel selection
    offspring = zeros(size(population));
    for j = 1:length(population)
        prob = fitness(j) / sum(fitness);
        offspring(j) = population(j) * prob;
    end

    % Update the population for the next generation
    population = offspring;
end

% Plot the frequency of the advantageous trait over time
plot(generations, population);
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Frequency of Advantageous Trait');
title('Natural Selection Simulation');
```

This code simulates a population of individuals with an advantageous trait that increases their fitness. The population evolves over time through natural selection, with individuals with the advantageous trait becoming more frequent in the population. The plot shows the frequency of the advantageous trait over time, demonstrating how natural selection can lead to the evolution of adaptive traits.

To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow for user input using the `Read-Host` cmdlet, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_host.ps1`) with the following content:
```powershell
param($input)

Write-Host "Hello, $input!"
```
1. In your Terraform configuration file (`.tf`), define a `local-exec` provisioner to execute the PowerShell script and pass the desired input:
```hcl
resource "null_resource" "example" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -File read_host.ps1 -input 'John Doe'"
  }
}
```
In this example, the `local-exec` provisioner will execute the `read_host.ps1` script and pass the input value of `John Doe`. You can replace `John Doe` with any other value you want to pass.

When you apply the Terraform configuration, the `local-exec` provisioner will execute the PowerShell script and display the output in the Terraform console.

Note that the `local-exec` provisioner is only available in Terraform versions 0.13 and later. If you're using an older version of Terraform, you may need to use a different provisioner, such as `shell` or `script`, to execute the PowerShell script.

