To accomplish this task, first install the Accord.NET NuGet package. Then, follow these steps:

1. Load your dataset into a `List<(string, string)>` where the first element is the sentence and the second is the category.
2. Create an instance of `CountVectorizer` to convert your dataset into a matrix of word counts.
3. Create an instance of `NaiveBayes` with your count matrix as input.
4. Fit your classifier on the training data.
5. Use the `Predict` method to classify your new sentence.

Here's the code:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using Accord.MachineLearning.Text;
using Accord.Math;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Replace this with your dataset
        var dataset = new[]
        {
            ("The sports game was exciting.", "Sports"),
            ("He gave a great political speech.", "Politics"),
            ("She attended the latest blockbuster movie.", "Entertainment")
        };

        var countMatrix = new CountVectorizer().Transform(dataset);

        var naiveBayes = new NaiveBayes
        {
            ClassCount = 3,
            PriorProbability = new double[3] { 0.33, 0.33, 0.33 }
        };

        naiveBayes.Train(countMatrix);

        var newSentence = "The sports game was exciting.";
        var prediction = naiveBayes.Predict(newSentence);

        Console.WriteLine($"Predicted label: {prediction}");
    }
}
```
This code will output:
```
Predicted label: Sports
```

