To create a socket for chatroom, you'll need to use PHP's built-in socket functions or a library like PHP-Socket. Here's an example using PHP-Socket:

1. Install PHP-Socket by running the following command in your terminal:
```bash
composer require php-socket/php-socket
```
1. Create a new PHP file (e.g., `chatroom_socket.php`) and add the following code:
```php
<?php
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';

use PhpSocket\Socket;

$host = 'localhost';
$port = 3000; // Choose a port number that's not in use
$socket = new Socket($host, $port, SOCK_STREAM);

if ($socket->connect()) {
    echo "Connected to the chatroom server!\n";

    // Send a message to the server
    $message = 'Hello, world!';
    $socket->send($message);

    // Receive messages from the server
    while (true) {
        $data = $socket->recv();
        if ($data === false) {
            break;
        }
        echo "Received message: {$data}\n";
    }

    $socket->close();
} else {
    echo "Unable to connect to the chatroom server.\n";
}
?>
```
1. Run the script using the following command:
```bash
php chatroom_socket.php
```
This script will connect to the chatroom server on port 3000 and send a message. It will then receive messages from the server and print them to the console.

Note that this is a basic example and you'll need to modify it to fit your specific chatroom application. Additionally, you'll need to ensure that the chatroom server is running and listening on the specified port.

Here's a function that sorts an array of integers in ascending order using the bubble sort algorithm:

```javascript
function bubbleSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;

  for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
      if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
        // Swap elements
        [arr[j], arr[j + 1]] = [arr[j + 1], arr[j]];
      }
    }
  }

  return arr;
}

const inputArray = [3, 7, 2, 1, 6];
const sortedArray = bubbleSort(inputArray);
console.log(sortedArray); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 6, 7]
```

